From: Husky <hyiy666@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] nss-drv: allow zero-usage samples in CPU load calculation

In idle or light-traffic scenarios on high-frequency NSS cores (e.g. 1.68GHz),
instruction count per millisecond (inst_cnt) is often around 1000-2000 instructions.
With max_ins = 1689600, integer division `(inst_cnt * 100) / max_ins` evaluates
to 0. The vendor check `|| (actual_usage == 0)` caused the driver to discard
these samples without incrementing avg_ctr, freezing cpu_load_ubi at its
last high-traffic historical value. Allow zero-usage samples to pass through
so that the rolling 1-second average smoothly decays to 0% in idle periods.

--- a/nss_freq.c
+++ b/nss_freq.c
@@ -144,7 +144,7 @@ static void nss_freq_compute_cpu_usage(struct nss_ctx_instance *nss_ctx, uint32_t inst_cnt)
 	 * TODO: To improve estimation algorithm for calculating how many actual instructions are executed.
 	 */
 	actual_usage = (inst_cnt * 100) / nss_freq_cpu_status.max_ins;
-	if ((actual_usage > NSS_FREQ_CPU_USAGE_MAX) || (actual_usage == 0)) {
+	if (actual_usage > NSS_FREQ_CPU_USAGE_MAX) {
 		spin_unlock_bh(&nss_freq_cpu_usage_lock);
 		return;
 	}
